Z||000018||JohnnyK||11-29-2000||06:28 PM||||No, I don't.. I've never seen ANYTHING of the sorts..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Well, except on VOlvo's, I have seen treble and bass clefs instead of the actual words.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Standard||11-30-2000||04:25 AM||||Well I got a bunch of questions for ya, PBS.  What does the Mr2 use for suspension?  Is it strut/strut or strut in rear, double wishbone in front or what?  I noticed that Mr2's also have the bump steer problem, the rear wheel toe's in when the suspension compresses.  Is it really bad?  Has there been any fix for it?  Are Mk I's and Mk II's completly different, as far as suspension design goes?<P>The Fiero uses a chapmann strut on the rear, and the double wishbone up front on 84-87 models, with the design improving a little as the years went on.  88's could be considered a Mk I.5, with a completely re-designed (actually what was originally planned to be used, before the budget cutters got to it) suspension.  3-link rear and redesigned double wishbone up front.  Sorry if you already knew this, I'm just bored  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 153,000 miles and some busted up ground f/x  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/redface.gif"> <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000020||PearlBlueSoul||11-30-2000||10:03 AM||||I actually didn't know that you guys had double wishbone.  I envy you now intensley!  hehe.  That setup actually INCREASES negative camber in hard cornering, whereas our setup increases positive camber.<P>About the toe issue, in the rear it is designed to help combat wheelspin.  When you accelerate, that 'squat' actually causes the rear tires to bite, allowing you to leave the line quicker.  I've only been able to chirp the tires in my n/A in first gear, even when I revved up to where my max torque is at 4500 (88.4 lb ft). <P>The design of the suspension is a coil over type Macpherson setup all around.  Coils on top over a macpherson strut casing, with the original struts being rebuildable units from the factory.  You also can get inserts to fit in the stock sized strut casings, and I know Tokico, and KYB both make adjustable units for replacement (via a knob at the top of the strut.  manipulates internal shock valving and baffles to stiffen the strut).  <P>The setup on MR2s is quite simple.  I know of only a few things you can do to MR2s for the suspension, whereas I know of about 10 things for an Integra that you could do in the way of suspension stuff.  if I ever really wanted to go all out on handling, this is what I would get. <P>HKS Adjustable pillow ball upper strut hats-  Allows for the tuning of the camber via adjustments at the top of the strut. <P>Suspension Techniques adjustable sway bar kit-  This kit allows tuning of the sway bar, via different holes drilled into the ends of the bar itself.  Can be tuned for understeer, oversteer, or neutral handling.  Comes with polyurethane bushings and adjustable rear end links.<P>Ground Control adjustable spring conversion (slip and weld-on kit)- for adjustments of the spring rates and ride height.  For a drop greater than 2" with this kit, you must get the weld on kit that GC sells.  <P>Tokico Illumina 5 way adjustable struts-  this long word basically means you can set the dampening rate between 5 settings, raning from softer to harder.  I would more than likely run it a tad stiffer in the back to help combat the extra weight imposed by the motor and to combat squat on acceleration.<P>Strut tower brace:  Helps to maintain chassis rigidity during a corner.  The struts will actually 'bend' in cornering, causing un-needed chassis flex and a waste of weight distribution during.  This brace helps to lock in the tops of the struts to maintain their original geometry, thereby allowing the suspension to put more load to the tires and increase the friction circle the tires have on the road.<P>*whew*!  hehe.  If you've ever looked at my style of posting, I do this quite often when people ask me, "what is that?".  Just thought I'd give you a taste of how I post on tech topics.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Hope this helps some.<BR><P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Justin91||11-30-2000||07:44 PM||||PBS, represent!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Thought you might like to see a familiar name away from home, and here I was gonna try to answer some questions.  But you're doing such a fantastic job, I think I'll just let you do all the grunt work!  Haha!  Oh well.  I don't know much about the mechanics, anyway.  But I do have a question about the Fiero.  Fieros are rare where I live (about as rare as MR2s), so I don't know anything about them.  I do know that every one around here is red, though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  How can I tell (without racing of course) if a Fiero is a V6 or not?  Does it say GT on the side?  I've seen one running around town that's been lowered and has an aftermarket spoiler and I think some ground effects, too.  Pretty cool looking car.  I just wanted to know how to tell if it's a GT, since I haven't noticed any such markings.  Thanks!<P>"At least they've got licenses: Drivers kill more deer than hunters."          <P>------------------<BR>Justin<p>[This message has been edited by Justin91 (edited 11-30-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000022||AL||11-30-2000||08:16 PM||||justin 91 there are markings on all fieros some say se or gt on the windows and some have it in the rear,but if no marking look for the dual exhuast in the back that wil tell you it's a v6,now if you see a car like mine then you will know that it has a v6 or v8 in some cars.<BR><B>AL_</B> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AL_sig.gif"> <BR>rear sway bar ported and polished heads,ported manifolds,flowmaster (80series) exhuast,holley side scoop and deck lid scoop,k&n air filter.<P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000023||PearlBlueSoul||11-30-2000||08:22 PM||||Heehheh....One more person, WOO HOO!!  <P>Suprisingly enough, Justin, not many other people besides ArmandoP, Velocifero, and myself have decided to come over (in peace).  I know there was someone else but it was to come over here and try to cram it down about how MR2s are the sheeat.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  Glad to see you're over here!  heh.  Call on more people, are home board Vets to come on over.  the water's fine!<P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Justin91||11-30-2000||10:33 PM||||Thanks for the explanation, Al.  The car looks almost exactly like yours and I do remember it having dual exhaust, so now I know not to race him <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.  PBS, I don't know if I'll be posting here very much or not.  I'd kinda like to, but I still don't know squat, so I wouldn't know how to carry on an intelligent conversation unless it was about general stuff.  We shall see...<P><I>Fattest newspaper ever printed: New York Times, October 17, 1965.  It had 946 pages and weighed 7-1/2 lbs.</I><P>    <P>------------------<BR>Justin<P>  <IMG SRC="http://board.mr2.com/unsorted/PIC00093edited3.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://board.mr2.com/unsorted/PIC00091edited3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000025||Black88GT||11-30-2000||11:21 PM||||Dual exhaust doesn't necessarily mean a V6.  I think the best way is listen to the engine if possible.  If it has a deep tone rumble, its a V6.  If it has a farty sound, its an Iron Puke.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">       <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||12
